  "supersededNote": "SUPERSEDED by Phase 6 (DEC-592). Do not regress against this file. It measured Phase 2a's harness — a star field and an orbit control, with no card tier, no thumbnails, no plane labels and no camera rig — over a six-segment path, on an 83-plane roster. `/bench` now flies the shipped scene over a nine-segment path at 87 planes, so not one segment here has a counterpart there and the two are not comparable in either direction. Kept because it is the record of what Phase 2a's renderer cost on its own, which is still the right thing to consult when the star field alone changes.",
  "note": "Phase 2a baseline on the reference machine (implementation-plan.md section 6). Closes PRD open question 7's first data point. `vsync` is the protocol run; `uncapped` adds --disable-gpu-vsync to show the real headroom, because a 120 Hz panel reports 8.3 ms whether the frame cost 2 ms or 8. Re-measured on `main` after the Phase 2a merge (DEC-628), which is the DEC-605 carry-forward: these are the first numbers taken against both the corrected fixture colour distribution and the merged tree, so they supersede the pre-merge run of the same date, which is in git history. No regression: the vsync run is vsync-bound at 120 fps either way, and uncapped moved 706 -> 716 fps with p95 unchanged at 2.8 ms and the worst frame 5.8 -> 4.7 ms. One earlier uncapped run on this same tree recorded a single 399.7 ms frame in the `approach` segment; it did not reproduce on a quiet machine (the run recorded here) and is attributed to concurrent load during that measurement rather than to the renderer. The bench drives Phase 2a's harness via `?bench`, which is one of the flags `App`'s router selects it on.",
